# ======================================================================================
# SilkGrain - production environment
#
# This is the template for /srv/silkgrain/shared/.env on the VPS. That file is the only
# place secrets live: mode 0600, owned by deploy, symlinked into every release. It is
# never copied into a release directory, because a release directory is what a rollback
# swaps, and a rollback that restored a superseded secret would take the shop down with
# an authentication failure nobody would think to look for.
#
#   sudo -u deploy install -m 0600 /dev/null /srv/silkgrain/shared/.env
#   sudo -u deploy nano /srv/silkgrain/shared/.env      # paste this, then fill it in
#
# Every variable down to the S3 block is one the API actually reads: the list is
# `EnvSchema` in apps/api/src/env.ts and nothing else. `loadEnv` parses it once at boot
# and throws on the first problem, so a wrong value here is a process that refuses to
# start rather than a checkout that fails at the till. Thirty-six variables; thirteen of
# them have no default and the process will not boot without them.
#
# Five more, under "Off-site backups", are read by `deploy/backup-db.sh` and by nothing
# in the API. They are here rather than in a file of their own because this is already
# the only 0600 deploy-owned file on the box, and a second credentials file is a second
# thing to forget to rotate and a second thing to leak.
#
# `.env.example` carries twelve more that nothing reads any more. They are listed at the
# bottom under "Deliberately absent" with the reason, so nobody copies them across and
# then wonders why changing one has no effect.
#
# Every marker means what it says:
#
#   [must differ]  the development value is wrong here, and several of them are wrong in
#                  a way that is silent. Read the note before copying anything.
#   [copy]         identical to development on purpose. Copy it.
#
# The domain is `silkgrain.com`, which is the real one (question Q-11, decision D-48).
# It is written out once per occurrence and never in a compound, so a different domain
# is still one command:
#
#   sed -i 's/silkgrain\.com/therealdomain.com/g' /srv/silkgrain/shared/.env
#
# The same is true of the Nginx site and DEPLOY.md, so the one sed per file is the whole
# change. `S3_PUBLIC_URL` below carries the domain too, and it has to agree with the
# `/media/` block in the Nginx site - see decision D-52 and the S3 section.
# ======================================================================================

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime
#
# NODE_ENV, API_HOST and API_PORT are ALSO pinned by ecosystem.config.cjs, and PM2's copy
# is the one the API process sees: `apps/api/src/config/dotenv.ts` calls dotenv with
# `override: false`, so anything already in the environment wins over this file. They are
# still listed here, with their production values, because the deploy runs `pnpm
# db:migrate` outside PM2 and that process reads this file and nothing else - and because
# a value that is correct in only one of the two places is a trap for whoever reads the
# other one first.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [must differ] The single switch that turns this into a production process. It sets HSTS
# on API responses, stops mounting Swagger UI at /docs (the document is a map of every
# request body the API accepts), drops the pino-pretty transport so logs are JSON lines
# PM2 can rotate and a parser can read, turns on `trustProxy: 1`, and makes `loadEnv`
# refuse both an insecure refresh cookie and the Stripe placeholders. It is also the only
# thing standing between this database and `pnpm db:seed` or `pnpm db:reset`: both read it
# and refuse outright, the seed because a fresh production database is empty and seeding
# one would create an owner account holding a password published in this repository, the
# reset because the production database is also called `silkgrain` and the name check
# alone would wave a DROP over every table the shop owns. Recovery in production is
# restoring the pre-deploy dump (decision D-17), never a reset.
NODE_ENV=production

# [must differ] Development runs `debug`, which logs every request and every query. On a
# shop taking orders that fills the disk PM2 writes to and buries the one 5xx line you
# came looking for. `silent` is never right either: the error line and its `requestId`
# are the only trace an incident leaves.
LOG_LEVEL=info

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# API
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [must differ] The default is 0.0.0.0 and that default is dangerous here, not merely
# untidy. `apps/api/src/app.ts` sets `trustProxy: 1` in production (decision D-39), which
# tells Fastify that exactly one proxy stands in front and that `request.ip` is therefore
# the last entry Nginx appended to X-Forwarded-For. If port 3001 is reachable from the
# internet, a client can connect to it directly, write its own X-Forwarded-For, and be
# handed a fresh rate-limit bucket on every request - which defeats every limiter in
# plugins/security.ts at once, including the ten-attempts-per-fifteen-minutes on admin
# sign-in that is the back office's only defence against online password guessing.
# Three locks, not one: this line, the same value pinned in ecosystem.config.cjs where a
# file on the server cannot get it wrong, and `ufw deny 3001`.
API_HOST=127.0.0.1

# [copy] Must match `proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3001` in the Nginx site and the PM2 app
# in ecosystem.config.cjs. Three places, one number; changing it means changing all three
# in the same commit.
API_PORT=3001

# [must differ] The storefront's public origin. It is pasted into every transactional
# email as the link back to the shop (apps/api/src/modules/mail/email.queue.ts), so a
# `localhost` value here mails customers a link to their own machine and an `http://` one
# mails them a link that redirects. No trailing slash: the templates append their own.
PUBLIC_WEB_URL=https://silkgrain.com

# [must differ] Validated at boot and, as of today, read by nothing - the back office is
# served from the same origin under /admin and never needs its own absolute URL. It stays
# in the schema because it is the value anything reaching for an admin link would want,
# and a variable that is validated is a variable that cannot be quietly wrong.
PUBLIC_ADMIN_URL=https://silkgrain.com/admin

# [must differ] Comma-separated allow-list, never `*`. Both SPAs are served from the same
# origin as the API in this topology, so a browser sends no preflight for them and a wrong
# value here fails silently in ordinary use, surfacing only for something genuinely
# cross-origin. Never `*` regardless: the refresh cookie makes every request credentialed,
# and @fastify/cors will not pair a wildcard with credentials anyway.
CORS_ORIGINS=https://silkgrain.com

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [must differ] Production MySQL 8 is on the standard 3306. The 3307 in development exists
# only because XAMPP owns 3306 on the developer's machine; nothing about the application
# wants a non-standard port.
#
# Not `root`. A user with rights on this schema and nothing else, so a SQL injection that
# got past Drizzle - or a leaked .env - reaches one database rather than the server.
#
# URL-encode the password. A `@`, `:`, `/`, `?` or `#` in it does not fail loudly; it
# reparses the URL into a different host and the API tries to connect somewhere that does
# not exist. `node -e "console.log(encodeURIComponent(process.argv[1]))" 'thepassword'`.
#
# The server's sql_mode must include STRICT_TRANS_TABLES and ONLY_FULL_GROUP_BY, which
# stock MySQL 8 does by default. Both are load-bearing: the audit writer relies on an
# over-long string being an error rather than a truncation (decision D-34).
DATABASE_URL=mysql://silkgrain:replace-me-url-encoded@127.0.0.1:3306/silkgrain

# [must differ, unless the box has four cores] A pool per process, and PM2 runs the API
# in cluster mode with `instances: 'max'`. The connections MySQL sees are this number times
# the core count, plus whatever mysqldump takes during the nightly backup, plus a second
# copy of the fleet for the few seconds a zero-downtime reload overlaps. Ten on four cores
# is forty against a default max_connections of 151 and is comfortable. Ten on sixteen
# cores is 160 and the API starts refusing connections under exactly the load that caused
# it. Divide, do not hope: (max_connections - 30) / (cores * 2), rounded down.
DATABASE_POOL_SIZE=10

# [must differ if Redis has a password, which it should] Bind Redis to loopback and set
# `requirepass`; the URL form is redis://:PASSWORD@127.0.0.1:6379 - note the empty user
# before the colon, and URL-encode the password for the same reason as above.
#
# `maxmemory-policy` must be `noeviction`. BullMQ keeps job state in Redis, so any
# evicting policy silently discards queued order-confirmation emails under memory
# pressure - the customer is charged, the order is right, and the receipt never arrives,
# with nothing in any log to say so.
REDIS_URL=redis://:replace-me-url-encoded@127.0.0.1:6379

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auth
#
# Generate each one separately, and do not reuse anything:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [must differ] Signs the fifteen-minute access token.
#
# `loadEnv` refuses this value in EVERY environment, not only production, on two counts:
# fewer than 32 characters, or anything beginning with `replace-me`. So the process will
# not boot until it is generated - which is the intended behaviour and is worth knowing at
# 3am, because the error names the variable and says "Still the placeholder from
# .env.example" rather than anything about booting.
#
# Rotating it logs every session out at once. That is what you want after a compromise and
# not what you want on a Tuesday afternoon.
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=replace-me-with-48-random-bytes-base64url

# [must differ] Keys the HMAC-SHA256 digest that refresh tokens are stored under (decision
# D-15): the token itself is 48 random bytes and never a JWT, and the database holds only
# the digest. Same two refusals as above, plus a third - `loadEnv` refuses it if it equals
# JWT_ACCESS_SECRET, because a leaked access secret must not be able to mint refreshes.
#
# Rotating this one invalidates every stored digest, so every refresh family stops
# matching and every customer and administrator is signed out.
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=replace-me-with-a-different-48-random-bytes

# [copy] Fifteen minutes is also the stale-privilege window (decision D-32): a demoted
# administrator keeps the role in their existing token until it expires and the refresh
# re-reads the row. Lengthening this lengthens that window. The four /team routes are the
# exception and re-read the database on every request.
JWT_ACCESS_TTL=15m

# [copy] The refresh cookie's lifetime and the row's expiry, kept in step.
JWT_REFRESH_TTL=30d

# [copy] Empty on purpose, and it is the correct production value for this topology.
#
# Empty means no Domain attribute, which means a host-only cookie: it goes back to exactly
# the host that set it and nowhere else. Naming the apex here would widen it in BOTH
# directions - silkgrain.com would send the refresh cookie to every subdomain, and every
# subdomain could SET a cookie the apex reads. Secure does not prevent that; a sibling
# subdomain over HTTPS can do it, and Path=/api/auth means the cookie it could toss is the
# session one. This deployment happens to have no sibling host - D-52 serves the images from
# the apex itself under /media/ rather than from media.silkgrain.com - but that is a fact
# about today's topology and not a reason to widen the cookie: the first subdomain anybody
# adds would inherit the session without anybody deciding to.
#
# The back office is /admin on the same origin, so widening buys nothing. Set the apex here
# only on the day the panel moves to admin.silkgrain.com, as a deliberate change.
COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# [must differ] `loadEnv` refuses `false` when NODE_ENV=production, and the refusal is the
# point: without it the refresh cookie travels in clear on any request that reaches http.
COOKIE_SECURE=true

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Orders
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [copy] Decision D-3, `SG-YYYY-NNNNN`. Two to four capital letters; the rest of the format
# is not configurable. The sequence is scoped to prefix and year - the allocator looks for
# the highest existing `SG-2026-%` - so changing this mid-year restarts numbering at 00001
# under the new prefix and leaves the shop with two live series and no way to say which
# order somebody means. Change it before the first order or never.
ORDER_NUMBER_PREFIX=SG

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe
#
# Decision D-27: `POST /api/checkout/intent` is not built, because nobody has ever run
# this application against a real Stripe account. What IS built and tested is the webhook
# at /api/webhooks/stripe - signature verification is local HMAC, so it could be proved
# offline.
#
# Decision D-51: if you do not have a Stripe account yet, set PAYMENTS_ENABLED=false and
# leave the three keys below exactly as they are. That is a supported deployment - a
# complete, indexable catalogue with no till - and it is the ONLY supported way to launch
# without keys.
#
# Do not instead put these placeholders in a production file with payments on. Verification
# is HMAC against STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET, `whsec_replace_me` is published in this repository,
# and an API holding it accepts a forged `payment_intent.succeeded` from anyone who can read
# GitHub: orders marked paid, stock decremented, receipts sent. `loadEnv` refuses the
# placeholders under NODE_ENV=production for exactly that reason, and inventing a
# realistic-looking string to get past it buys nothing - there is no checkout to use it.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [copy, or set false] Whether this deployment takes money. `false` does not register the
# webhook route at all, and makes the three keys below unnecessary. Default is `true`, so
# an operator who simply forgot the keys gets a loud refusal at boot rather than a shop
# that quietly cannot be paid.
PAYMENTS_ENABLED=true

# [must differ] The live secret key, `sk_live_...`, from Dashboard > Developers > API keys
# with test mode off. `loadEnv` refuses any value containing `replace_me` when
# NODE_ENV=production. This one is a real credential: it can move money and read every
# customer record in the account, and it belongs in this file and nowhere else.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_replace_me

# [must differ] The signing secret of the endpoint YOU create in the dashboard, pointed at
# https://silkgrain.com/api/webhooks/stripe. It is not the one `stripe listen` prints
# in development - those are different secrets for different endpoints, and a mismatch is
# a 400 on every event, which Stripe retries for three days before giving up. Since an
# order only becomes `paid` in the webhook (never on the redirect), three days of that
# means three days of customers who were charged and whose orders still read `pending`.
#
# The route needs the body exactly as sent: no proxy rewrite, no re-encoding, no
# `sub_filter` on that location.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_replace_me

# [must differ] The publishable key, `pk_live_...`. Named with the VITE_ prefix because
# the storefront needs the same value at build time and the API hands it back in the
# checkout response - two copies of one key is one copy too many. It is public by
# definition: it ends up in a JavaScript bundle any visitor can read, and Stripe issues it
# on that understanding. See BUILD-TIME below for how it reaches the bundle, which is not
# by being in this file.
VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_live_replace_me

# [copy] Decision D-4. Validated at boot, read by nothing today: task 4.6 is the other
# half of D-27 and is not started, so the cart's "Estimated Tax" is still the default rate
# from the settings table. Leave it true so that turning Stripe Tax on is a code change
# and not also a configuration hunt.
STRIPE_TAX_ENABLED=true

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email
#
# Development points at Mailpit, which accepts anything, delivers nowhere and has an HTTP
# API the tests read. Production points at a real provider. Which provider is question
# Q-41 and is unanswered; whichever it is, it speaks SMTP and nothing in the code changes.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [must differ] The provider's SMTP host.
SMTP_HOST=replace-me.smtp.example

# [must differ] 587 with SMTP_SECURE=false, or 465 with SMTP_SECURE=true. Nothing else.
SMTP_PORT=587

# [must differ] This is implicit TLS, not "use TLS at all". `false` on port 587 means
# nodemailer connects in clear and upgrades with STARTTLS, which is correct and is what
# every provider documents. `true` on 587 makes it open a TLS handshake against a plain
# socket, which does not fail - it hangs until the socket times out, and every queued
# email exhausts its retries with a timeout that says nothing about the cause.
SMTP_SECURE=false

# [must differ] The provider's username, often an API-key sentinel such as `apikey`.
# Leave both of these empty only for a relay that wants no credentials: the mailer omits
# authentication entirely when SMTP_USER is empty, so an empty user against a provider
# that requires one produces "relay access denied" rather than an authentication error.
SMTP_USER=replace_me
SMTP_PASSWORD=replace_me

# [copy] The display name on every message.
MAIL_FROM_NAME=SilkGrain

# [must differ] Must be on a domain with SPF and DKIM published for the chosen provider,
# and ideally DMARC. Without them the receipts land in spam, the customer concludes the
# order failed, and the first anyone hears of it is a support ticket about a double
# charge that never happened.
MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=orders@silkgrain.com

# [must differ] Where replies go, and also the support address printed in every email
# footer - the templates fall back to MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS when it is empty. Empty is
# allowed and means both.
MAIL_REPLY_TO=hello@silkgrain.com

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Object storage (product images)
#
# The values below are the one-box topology this project actually deploys (decision D-52):
# MinIO on the same VPS, bound to loopback, its bucket served to the browser by Nginx from
# the shop's own origin under /media/. Which hosted provider to use instead is the other
# half of question Q-41; any S3-compatible endpoint works unchanged, because the code
# touches only PutObject, DeleteObject, HeadBucket, CreateBucket and PutBucketPolicy.
#
# The two halves of that arrangement - S3_PUBLIC_URL here and `location ^~ /media/` in
# deploy/nginx/silkgrain.conf - have to agree about the domain AND the bucket name. Nothing
# checks it: a mismatch is a 404 on every product photograph and no other symptom.
#
# Moving to a hosted bucket later is this block, the `img-src` in both CSP headers of the
# Nginx site, and nothing else - no code, no migration. Rows already written keep the URL
# they were written with, so do it before there are images rather than after.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [must differ] Where the API writes. MinIO listens on loopback and the browser never
# reaches this address - it reads over S3_PUBLIC_URL instead, which is the whole reason the
# two are separate variables. Plain http is correct here and is not a downgrade: the hop is
# a unix-level loopback connection on the same machine, and `ufw` does not open 9000.
S3_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9000

# [copy] Part of the SigV4 signature. MinIO ignores it, real S3 does not, and a wrong
# region is a 400 with a message about the endpoint rather than about the region.
S3_REGION=us-east-1

# [copy, or differ] The bucket name is also the URI on the `proxy_pass` in the Nginx
# /media/ block. Change it in both places in the same commit. Against a hosted provider,
# do not reuse the development name either: bucket names are global on AWS and the failure
# is a confusing 403 rather than "already taken".
S3_BUCKET=silkgrain-media

# [must differ] MinIO's root credentials, generated in DEPLOY.md section 2.10 and written
# in exactly two files on the box - /etc/default/minio and this one.
#
# The credentials need PutObject and DeleteObject, and also HeadBucket, CreateBucket and
# PutBucketPolicy: Storage.provision creates the bucket and opens it for anonymous reads
# lazily, on the first upload. If a hosted key cannot write a bucket policy - which is
# normal on a locked-down account, and on R2 - create the bucket and its public-read policy
# by hand first and the lazy path becomes a no-op HeadBucket.
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=replace_me
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=replace_me

# [copy] True for MinIO, false for AWS S3 and for most clouds. The wrong value does not
# fail cleanly: path-style against a provider that wants virtual-host style is a 301 or a
# 403 on every upload, and neither mentions addressing style.
S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

# [must differ] The browser-facing base URL, and the one variable here that is hard to
# change later. Uploads store the ABSOLUTE URL in `product_images.url`, built from this
# value at upload time. Change it after images exist and two things happen: every existing
# row still points at the old host, and deleting one of those images silently does nothing,
# because `Storage.remove` recognises a key only inside the current prefix.
#
# No bucket name in it: the Nginx block adds `/silkgrain-media` on the way through, so the
# path the browser sees is /media/<key> and the bucket is an implementation detail of the
# box. Whatever this value is, its origin has to be allowed by `img-src` in the CSP - which
# for this topology is `'self'`, and needs no host listed at all.
S3_PUBLIC_URL=https://silkgrain.com/media

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Off-site backups - read by deploy/backup-db.sh, never by the API
#
# `loadEnv` never sees these. They are not in `EnvSchema`, and they should not be: no code
# path in the running process uploads a database dump. `backup-db.sh` reads this file
# directly, one key at a time, without sourcing it.
#
# Leaving them blank is a supported state and it means exactly one thing. With
# BACKUP_S3_BUCKET unset the script exits 3 - "the dump is complete and verified, the
# off-site copy is not" - and deploy.sh downgrades that to a warning and carries on,
# because what the next twenty minutes might need is a file on this disk and it is there.
# What you have then is every backup sitting on the disk you are afraid of losing.
#
# Nothing here expires a remote object. The 14-day rotation in the script is local only,
# deliberately: a rotation bug that can reach the off-site copies can delete every backup
# there has ever been. Expire the `database/` prefix with a bucket lifecycle rule instead,
# which is the provider's job and cannot be given the wrong argument at 3am.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# [must differ] A PRIVATE bucket, and it MUST NOT be S3_BUCKET. The API opens S3_BUCKET
# for anonymous s3:GetObject on first use - product images are public by definition, and
# modules/media/storage.service.ts writes that policy itself. A database dump in that
# bucket is the whole shop, customers and password hashes included, on a URL anybody can
# guess. The script refuses outright when the two names match; this comment is why.
#
# EMPTY, and deliberately so, in the one-box topology of decision D-52. The only S3
# endpoint on that box is the MinIO beside the database, so a "remote" copy would be a
# second file on the disk whose failure the backup exists to survive - which reads like
# off-site backup and is not one. Empty makes backup-db.sh exit 3, "the dump is complete
# and verified, the off-site copy is not", which deploy.sh downgrades to a warning.
#
# Fill this in with a bucket at a DIFFERENT provider the day one exists, and prefer a key
# that can write and not read.
BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=

# [copy, or differ] Each of the four below falls back to its `S3_` sibling when left
# empty, because a one-VPS deployment has one object-storage account and two copies of one
# endpoint is one copy too many. They exist separately so that moving the backups to
# another provider - or to a key that can write and not read, which is worth doing - is a
# change to this file and not to the script.
BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=
BACKUP_S3_REGION=
BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# ======================================================================================
# BUILD-TIME variables - these are NOT read from this file
#
# The two SPAs are static files. Whatever they need is compiled into their bundles by
# `pnpm build`, long before this .env is ever read, so a value here reaches them only if
# it was also in the environment of the build.
#
# Two facts that cost an afternoon each if they are not known:
#
#  1. Vite reads .env files from the app's own directory - apps/web and apps/admin - and
#     not from the repository root. deploy/deploy.sh links the shared .env into the release
#     root, which feeds `pnpm db:migrate`; Vite never looks there and never will. What Vite
#     DOES read is any `VITE_`-prefixed variable already in process.env, and those win over
#     its own files. So the way to hand a value to a build is to export that one variable:
#
#       export VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="$(sed -n \
#         's/^VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=//p' /srv/silkgrain/shared/.env)"
#
#     One variable, not `set -a; . shared/.env`: sourcing the whole file would put the
#     Stripe secret key and the database password into the environment of every build tool
#     and every postinstall script that runs under it, to hand Vite one public value.
#
#     deploy.sh does not do this today, deliberately - nothing in either bundle reads a
#     VITE_ variable yet, so it would be ceremony around a value nobody uses. It is what
#     the deploy grows when the checkout page lands, and it belongs in deploy.sh rather
#     than in a shell somebody remembers: a build run without it succeeds and ships a
#     bundle with `undefined` where the key should be.
#
#  2. Anything named VITE_* is PUBLIC. It is substituted into JavaScript that is served to
#     every visitor and cached at the edge for a year, and `curl` plus `grep` over
#     /assets/index-*.js is the whole of the attack. Nothing secret may ever be given this
#     prefix - and renaming a secret to get it into a bundle is not a workaround, it is
#     the same mistake with a different label.
#
# What the frontends read today: nothing. Neither app references `import.meta.env` at all
# (they call a relative `/api`, deliberately, so there is no base URL that can point a
# production bundle at a development API). `VITE_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` is the only
# VITE_-named variable in the schema, and today it is read by the API; it becomes a build
# input for the storefront when the checkout page lands with task 4.2.
# ======================================================================================

# ======================================================================================
# Deliberately absent
#
# These are in `.env.example` and no code reads any of them. They are named here so that
# nobody copies them across, sets one, and spends an evening wondering why it does
# nothing.
#
#   DATABASE_URL_TEST              the integration-test database. Read only by
#                                  apps/api/src/test/harness.ts, and it must not exist on a
#                                  production host at all: the harness TRUNCATEs every
#                                  mutable table in whatever it points at, with foreign-key
#                                  checks switched off, before it runs.
#   PAYPAL_ENV, PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID,  decision D-26 moved PayPal to BACKLOG.md. Stripe is the
#   PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET,          only provider.
#   PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID,
#   VITE_PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID
#   MAIL_TRANSPORT                 there is one transport, SMTP, chosen in mailer.ts.
#   MAIL_OPS_ADDRESS               superseded by the `ops.notification_email` row in the
#                                  settings table, editable in the back office.
#   FREE_SHIPPING_THRESHOLD_CENTS  decision D-22: `shipping_rates.free_above_cents` is the
#                                  authority, and the checkout charges from the rate row.
#   DEFAULT_TAX_RATE_BASIS_POINTS  superseded by `commerce.default_tax_basis_points` in the
#                                  settings table.
#   SENTRY_DSN, VITE_SENTRY_DSN    no SDK is installed and nothing reads these. Setting
#                                  them would report nothing while looking like it
#                                  reported something, which is worse than the gap. The
#                                  reasoning, and exactly where the hooks go on the day a
#                                  DSN exists, is in docs/observability.md.
# ======================================================================================
